{
  "id": "Compound::DB00959 - Disease::DOID:7148",
  "verbalization": "Methylprednisolone, a corticosteroid, is linked to Rheumatoid Arthritis through its inclusion in the class of corticosteroid hormone receptor agonists, which includes betamethasone and hydrocortisone. These compounds are known to modulate inflammatory pathways by binding to glucocorticoid receptors, thereby suppressing the activity of pro-inflammatory cells such as T-cells and macrophages. This mechanism is supported by the observed treatment of rheumatoid arthritis by betamethasone and hydrocortisone, both of which are structurally related to methylprednisolone. The shared pathway through 17Alpha-hydroxy steroid suggests a common biological mechanism involving the regulation of inflammatory responses.\n\nThe evidence from the paths highlights a consistent role of corticosteroids in modulating inflammatory processes, with methylprednisolone being a relevant member of this class. The low final score for path_186, which links methylprednisolone to gout and then to rheumatoid arthritis, indicates some uncertainty in the direct therapeutic effect on rheumatoid arthritis, but the overall evidence supports a mechanistic link through the corticosteroid receptor pathway. The integration of these findings suggests that methylprednisolone may exert its therapeutic effects on rheumatoid arthritis through the suppression of inflammatory responses, mediated by glucocorticoid receptor activation.",
